{
  "//": "§2.2 «ПК в пати». j = LP(\"AFKLINK-PARTY-JOIN-V1\") || LP(device_id) || LP(party_id 16) || LP(party UTF-8) || LP(leader.telegram_user_id decimal) || LP(leader.name or \"\") || LP(leader.username or \"\") || LP(key_id(leader.spki) 32) || LP(grant_token 16, or \"\") || LP(issued_at u64 BE ms) || LP(by 32); sig = ECDSA-P256-SHA256 r||s by the PC owner's device key. The PC answers with the first failed check of §4.2 (party_ack.result): malformed (form §2: v 1; party 1-64 code points, no control characters; leader name <= 128, username <= 64 Unicode code points (the unit the released grant schema counts in), null allowed; every text well-formed (no unpaired surrogate; a case with join_text carries the JSON text itself); leader.spki canonical; strict base64url; at most 2048 bytes of compact JSON, unknown fields counted and skipped), device (device_id not pc.device_id), signer (by not an active owner record of pc.keys; in strict mode also not unconfirmed), bad_sig (under the SPKI of by's record), stale (grant_token not pc.grant_token, byte for byte), else ok. pc is the PC's state; a case's pc overrides its fields. j_hex: the bytes the PC rebuilds from the case's JSON, and sig_valid: whether sig is by's signature over them (both absent where the JSON's signed fields were altered after signing). damaged, busy and limit depend on the PC beyond this state: scenarios. Consumers assert both the result (expect) and `why` of every negative case: several cases are built so that a verifier missing one rule reaches a different result, and `why` names the rule that must refuse first.",
